From estuart-4GNroTWusrE at public.gmane.org  Tue Oct 27 18:24:27 2015
From: estuart-4GNroTWusrE at public.gmane.org (Elizabeth Stuart)
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2015 18:24:27 +0000
Subject: Re: Specify groups in matchit function
In-Reply-To: <CAHb4TqvCAjR1c4BZ+UH5PCsuvNw+Ji71yt177bXknhmJQk5aoQ@mail.gmail.com>
References: <CAHb4TqvCAjR1c4BZ+UH5PCsuvNw+Ji71yt177bXknhmJQk5aoQ@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <84DDF7B0-CD27-4C81-8C87-B237B7ED2637@jhu.edu>

Hi Susan,
I’m not sure I understand your question but a few thoughts:

- The function should be giving you pairs, one of whom has Interv=0 and the other with Interv=1; I wonder if you are looking somehow only at the first member of each pair?   If you use the match.data() command after the matchit call it should give you back all of the subjects that were put into a pair (those with interv=1 and interv=0).

- I’m not sure I understand how you want to deal with center, but if you want each matched pair to have 2 people that came from the same center (what I would call an “exact match” on center; i.e., two individuals can only be matched if they are from the same center), you can do that using the “exact=“ option in the matchit call.

Liz





